B) For the population as a whole, annual alcohol consumption is no lower now than it was in 1990.\nThis option indicates that overall alcohol consumption has not decreased since 1990. This could explain why people under 21 continue to drink as much alcohol as they did before 1990, despite the decrease in alcohol-related traffic deaths among them. This option helps to resolve the discrepancy by providing a plausible explanation for the continued high alcohol consumption among people under 21.\n\nC) The proportion of people under 21 who own a car is higher now than it was in 1990.\nThis option suggests that more people under 21 own cars now than in 1990. While this could potentially lead to more opportunities for alcohol consumption, it does not directly address the discrepancy between alcohol consumption and traffic deaths among people under 21.\n\nD) Alcohol consumption away from home, for example in bars and restaurants, is much lower among people under 21 than it was in 1990.\nThis option indicates that alcohol consumption away from home has decreased among people under 21. While this could potentially lead to a decrease in alcohol-related traffic deaths among them, it does not directly address the discrepancy between alcohol consumption and traffic deaths among people under 21. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
